<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/7th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Hulao</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">621</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transition-from-sui-to-tang">Transition from Sui to Tang</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Hulao was fought in 621 at the Hulao Pass, a fortified gap west of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhengzhou">Zhengzhou</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henan">Henan</a>, during the wars that followed the fall of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sui-dynasty">Sui dynasty</a>. The Tang prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-taizong-of-tang">Li Shimin</a>, besieging <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wang-shichong">Wang Shichong</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a>, held the pass against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dou-jiande">Dou Jiande</a>'s relieving army, routed it and captured Dou. Wang Shichong surrendered soon after, and the victory gave the Tang control of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-china-plain">North China Plain</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-hulao.jpg" alt="Battle of Hulao" width="320" height="218" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: SS · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battle_of_Luoyang_Hulao.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>As the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sui-dynasty">Sui dynasty</a>'s authority collapsed under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-yang-of-sui">Emperor Yang</a>, local governors and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnates">magnates</a> rose to claim power. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-gaozu-of-tang">Li Yuan</a>, Duke of Tang, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a> in 617 and proclaimed himself first emperor of the Tang in 618, and in 618-620 his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-taizong-of-tang">Li Shimin</a> defeated the dynasty's rivals in the north-west. Two regimes held the north-eastern plain: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wang-shichong">Wang Shichong</a>, a former Sui general who had declared himself emperor of Zheng at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dou-jiande">Dou Jiande</a>, a one-time bandit leader who ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebei">Hebei</a> as King of Xia.</p><p>In August 620 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-taizong-of-tang">Li Shimin</a> marched on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a> with 50,000 men and ringed it with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fortified-camp">fortified camps</a>; most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henan">Henan</a> went over to the Tang, and two breakout attempts by Wang failed. By March 621 people in the city were eating cakes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> and mud. Wang appealed to Dou, who in April marched west with an army that the sources put at 100,000 or 120,000 men.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Against the advice of his older generals, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-taizong-of-tang">Li Shimin</a> left the siege to his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-yuanji">Li Yuanji</a> and the general Qutu Tong and took 3,500 men to the Hulao Pass, some 97 km east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a>, which he occupied on 22 April. For about a month Dou camped at Banzhu and offered battle, while Li Shimin waited in his strong position. Dou's official Ling Jing urged him to cross the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-river">Yellow River</a> and strike at the Tang heartland in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanxi">Shanxi</a>, but the Xia generals refused.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-taizong-of-tang">Li Shimin</a> feigned sending part of his cavalry away, and on the morning of 28 May Dou drew up his army along the Sishui river. The Tang held back until noon, when the Xia troops grew thirsty and broke ranks to fetch water. A probing attack of 300 horsemen under Yuwen Shiji found them in confusion, and a general assault followed. When Li Shimin and his cavalry broke through to the eastern escarpment, the Xia army collapsed: 3,000 men fell and more than 50,000 were captured, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dou-jiande">Dou Jiande</a> himself.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wang-shichong">Wang Shichong</a> surrendered on 4 June after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-taizong-of-tang">Li Shimin</a> showed him the captured Dou and his generals, and the Xia surrendered on 10 June. Dou was executed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a>, and Wang was killed on his way into exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sichuan">Sichuan</a>. The historian David A. Graff called the battle the single most decisive engagement of the civil wars after the fall of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sui-dynasty">Sui</a>. The last rebel leader, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liang-shidu">Liang Shidu</a>, was defeated in June 628.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Hulao” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Li Shimin blocked him at the <b>Hulao</b> pass and captured him; he was executed at Chang'an.</blockquote><a href="/state/xia-dou-jiande">Xia (Dou Jiande)</a></li><li><blockquote>After he defeated and captured the relieving army of Dou Jiande at <b>Hulao</b> in 621, Wang Shichong surrendered.</blockquote><a href="/state/zheng-wang-shichong">Zheng (Wang Shichong)</a></li></ul></section>
